Most small businesses start automating on a free plan, and the four best-known options draw their free lines in very different places. Zapier limits tasks and the number of steps, Make gives you more credits but only two live scenarios, Zoho Flow limits flows and tasks, and n8n sells no free cloud plan at all but lets you self-host its Community edition for the cost of a server. This guide sets out what each vendor publishes about its free option so you can see which one fits a small job before you pay for anything. It does not judge ease of use or reliability, because we have not run these tools side by side. A disclosure: this post was drafted by Claude, an AI assistant made by Anthropic, and checked by a separate AI reviewer against the vendor pages cited below, not by using the products ourselves. At the time of writing, Stackbrook has no affiliate relationship with Zapier, Make, Zoho or n8n. For how each tool counts usage once you pay, see our guides to [Zapier vs Make pricing](/posts/zapier-vs-make-pricing) and [n8n vs Zapier vs Make](/posts/n8n-vs-zapier-vs-make).

## The free plans at a glance

| | Zapier Free | Make Free | Zoho Flow Free | n8n Community |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Allowance | 100 tasks a month | 1,000 credits a month | 100 tasks a month | Free to self-host |
| Workflow limit | Unlimited Zaps, but two-step only | 2 active scenarios (reported) | 5 flows | Set by your server |
| Schedule limit | 15-minute checks for new data | 15-minute minimum interval | 15-minute polling | Your own settings |
| Hosting | Zapier's cloud | Make's cloud | Zoho's cloud | You host it |

![Table-style diagram of free automation plans, with Zapier 100 tasks and two-step Zaps, Make 1,000 credits and a reported 2 active scenarios, Zoho Flow 100 tasks and 5 flows, n8n Community free to self-host](/images/site1/free-automation-plans-compared-chart.svg)
*Chart created by Stackbrook from Zapier's, Make's, Zoho's and n8n's published pages.*

## Zapier Free

Zapier's pricing page lists a free plan at $0 with 100 tasks a month and unlimited Zaps, Tables and Forms, but limits Zaps to two steps, a trigger and one action. Multi-step Zaps, webhooks and email support appear on the paid Professional plan, which Zapier's blog lists at $19.99 a month billed annually for 750 tasks.

Zapier's blog says a task is the work Zapier does in another app, and that triggers, filters, paths, formatting and looping do not count. On a two-step Zap that means one action per run, so the free plan covers roughly 100 runs a month. Zapier's pricing page lists paid tiers above 100 tasks, and offers a 14-day trial of Professional.

## Make Free

Make's pricing page lists a Free plan at $0 with 1,000 credits a month, a visual builder, and routers and filters. Make's pricing FAQ says most module actions use one credit, so a three-module scenario uses about three credits a run, and credits expire at the end of each term.

The limits sit elsewhere. Make's Academy says the minimum interval between scheduled runs on the free subscription is 15 minutes, and Make's file-handling help page lists a 5 MB file size cap on Free. A reply in a Make community thread says the free plan allows only 2 active scenarios, and third-party summaries report the same, but we could not confirm it on Make's live pricing page, so check it before you plan around it. Paid plans list higher limits. If you use Make's built-in AI tools on Free, Make's documentation notes a weekly limit of 200,000 input tokens, and credits for AI use depend on the tokens processed and the model chosen.

## Zoho Flow Free

Most of Zoho's pages say Zoho Flow has a free plan. Its pricing page, its operational automation FAQ and its main product page all say the free plan includes up to 5 flows and 100 tasks a month, and the pricing page lists 30-day flow history, 15-minute polling, base apps and logic tools on Free. One outlier, a Zoho guide written for people migrating from Make, says Zoho Flow has no permanent free tier, only a 15-day trial, and the pricing page also advertises a 15-day trial of the premium features. Check the signup page for the plan you will actually be offered.

Paid Zoho Flow plans are priced by monthly task allowance, per organisation and not per user, according to Zoho. Zoho's comparison pages list Standard from $25 a month billed annually and Professional at $41 a month for 10,000 tasks, with Standard starting at 5,000 tasks. Zoho's pricing page did not show dollar figures when we read it, so confirm them there. Zoho also says Flow is included in Zoho One, which matters if you already use Zoho's other apps.

## n8n

n8n has no ongoing free cloud plan. Its pricing page lists Starter at €20 a month billed annually for 2,500 workflow executions and Pro at €50 for 10,000, with a free trial for the hosted plans (n8n's comparison page describes it as 14 days). Every plan includes unlimited workflows and users, and an execution is one run of a whole workflow whatever its length.

The free route is the Community edition. n8n says you can deploy it on your own server at no extra cost and only pay for the infrastructure, and its support pages say it runs under the Sustainable Use License, and that you can use the Community edition for free if only you or people in your organisation create or edit workflows. Outsiders building workflows, hosting n8n as a service or white-labelling it need a commercial licence, so read n8n's licence article before you build anything for clients on it. Self-hosting also means you handle updates, backups and security yourself, and a server is a real monthly cost, so it is only free in the sense that n8n charges nothing for the software.

## How to choose

- **A tiny job with two steps:** Zapier Free can cover a trigger plus one action, up to 100 runs a month.
- **More runs, a few live workflows:** Make's 1,000 credits go further, but a reported limit of two active scenarios applies and runs happen at most every 15 minutes.
- **You already use Zoho apps:** Zoho Flow's free tier or trial is worth testing, once you confirm what your signup offers.
- **Many workflows and you can run a server:** n8n Community is not metered by n8n's cloud plans, but you take on the hosting and should read its licence terms.

Whichever you pick, count your own runs and steps first, since the free limits are tight, and revisit the vendor pages before relying on any figure here.
